    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-root",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "🧩 Edge Class Contract (Normative)",
      "position": {
        "x": 140,
        "y": 160
      },
      "width": 780,
      "height": 320,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Edge Class Contract\n\n**Authority:** Normative\n\nAn edge class is an optional reusable semantic upgrade for a plain edge. It may own relationship meaning, light rendering defaults, applicability constraints, and legend identity. A plain edge remains fully valid without a class.\n\nThis contract protects execution without turning edge authoring into ceremony:\n\n- diagnostics report broken declared promises\n- optional capabilities remain silent when absent\n- templates generate richer, well-connected edge-class graphs for new work\n- older lean edge classes are not warned merely for lacking decorative or explanatory structure"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-identity",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "1. Identity (Required)",
      "position": {
        "x": -260,
        "y": 520
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## 1. Identity Contract (Required)\n\nA new edge-class artifact uses one authority-bearing declaration with:\n\n- `data.declaration.kind: edge-class`\n- a canonical declared `root` surface\n- `data.edgeClass.key`: stable semantic key, such as `idea.supports`\n- `data.edgeClass.label`: human-facing label\n- `data.edgeClass.meaning`: concise relationship meaning\n\nThe declaration-owned `data.edgeClass` object is the canonical semantic home. Legacy Field-node representations may continue loading as compatibility data, but new templates should not split one edge-class contract across required Field nodes.\n\nDiagnostics should report a missing or duplicate promised key, an unresolved root, or malformed declared identity. They should not demand optional views, examples, constraints, or style fields."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-render",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "2. Render Defaults (Required)",
      "position": {
        "x": 300,
        "y": 520
      },
      "width": 620,
      "height": 420,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## 2. Rendering Defaults (Optional Capability)\n\nAn edge class may declare `data.edgeClass.defaultEdgeType` and a small `data.edgeClass.renderDefaults` object.\n\nUseful render defaults include:\n\n- `stroke`\n- `width`\n- `dash`\n- `routingHint`\n- `labelTone`\n\nWhen declared, these values are the class defaults used by canvas rendering and previews. Edge-instance overrides remain authoritative for that instance.\n\nAbsence of render defaults is valid and silent. Invalid declared values, an unknown declared edge type, or contradictory render promises are diagnostics."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-preview",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "3. Preview (Required Behavior)",
      "position": {
        "x": 960,
        "y": 520
      },
      "width": 620,
      "height": 420,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## 3. Preview Behavior\n\nA template should create a useful root or summary presentation that shows the edge label, key, meaning, and a representative line. The Idea template edge classes are the current compact reference pattern.\n\nA preview is not a universal validity requirement. When a class declares both render defaults and a canonical preview, the preview should reflect those defaults. Demonstrable drift between two declared representations is a broken promise; absence of an optional preview is not a warning."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-views",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "4. Views (Recommended Minimum)",
      "position": {
        "x": -260,
        "y": 880
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 380,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## 4. Surface Contract\n\nEvery new edge-class graph follows the base graph contract and exposes one canonical `root` surface.\n\nThe root should make the class understandable and addressable. It may use `node.web.summary`, as the Idea edge classes do.\n\nAdditional payloads such as `node.web.icon`, `node.web.detail`, `node.web.document`, or `editor.web` are optional capabilities. If declared, they must resolve and honor their payload contract. If absent, validators remain silent."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-export",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "5. Export Surface (Library Declaration)",
      "position": {
        "x": 300,
        "y": 980
      },
      "width": 620,
      "height": 420,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## 5. Export and Consumption\n\nAn edge class only needs an export contract when it claims library consumption.\n\nA library that exports edge classes must expose them through a declared export surface using allow or allowlist policy. A consuming graph must use a real bridge with appropriate authority and select the intended export surface when selection is required.\n\nReachability alone does not grant class authority. A local template may instead bridge directly to its edge-class artifacts, as the Idea template does.\n\nDiagnostics report broken declared exports, unresolved class references, or invalid bridge authority. A private or local edge class that never claims library export does not receive an export warning."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-assignment",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "6. Assignment + Precedence",
      "position": {
        "x": 960,
        "y": 980
      },
      "width": 620,
      "height": 420,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## 6. Edge Assignment and Precedence\n\nA plain edge remains valid without a class.\n\nWhen an edge is classified, it should store:\n\n- `edge.data.edgeClassRef`: durable semantic authority\n- `edge.data.edgeClassKey`: stable key mirror\n\nRendering precedence is:\n\n1. explicit edge-instance overrides\n2. declared class defaults\n3. the ordinary plain-edge fallback\n\nThe class owns reusable meaning. The edge instance owns its endpoints and may own local display metadata or rendering overrides."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "edge-class-contract-step-2-checklist",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Step 2 Checklist (Concrete)",
      "position": {
        "x": 140,
        "y": 1440
      },
      "width": 1080,
      "height": 520,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## New Edge-Class Template Checklist\n\nTemplates should create the richer graph; users should not have to retrofit it manually.\n\n### Required executable core\n\n- declaration with `kind: edge-class`\n- canonical declared `root` surface backed by a real port\n- `data.edgeClass.key`\n- `data.edgeClass.label`\n- `data.edgeClass.meaning`\n\n### Strong generated defaults\n\n- `defaultEdgeType`\n- small `renderDefaults`\n- optional `sourceKinds` and `targetKinds`\n- readable root preview\n- structural edge from declaration to root\n- an example relationship when the template family benefits from one\n- semantic explanatory edges where they clarify meaning\n\n### Validation boundary\n\n- reject unresolved or contradictory declared values\n- validate source and target constraints only when declared\n- validate preview consistency only when both preview and render defaults are declared\n- require export behavior only when library export is claimed\n- never warn solely because optional richness is absent\n\nPass criteria: the class resolves through its root, classified edges retain their endpoints and meaning, and every capability the class claims actually works."
      }
    }
  ],
